# Workspace Analysis

[← Back to Tensegrity Robot docs](README.md) · [Project root](../../README.md)

Workspace analysis characterises the reachable Cartesian space of the
tensegrity manipulator and compares it against conventional robot arms
(UR10e 6-DOF, Kinova Gen3 7-DOF).  The analysis uses **Monte Carlo
forward-kinematics sampling** to estimate reachability, manipulability,
and isotropy across the task-relevant volume.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[workspace_config.py<br/>Robot configs, bounds, defaults] --> B[workspace_sample.py<br/>Monte Carlo FK sampling]
    B -->|NumPy files| C[workspace_visualize.py<br/>3-D voxel scatter + 2-D heatmaps]
    B -->|NumPy files| D[workspace_analysis.ipynb<br/>Interactive analysis]
    E[workspace_analysis_helper.py<br/>Shared data loading & binning] -.-> C & D
```

---

## 1  Methodology

### 1.1  Monte Carlo Forward-Kinematics Sampling

Joint configurations are **uniformly sampled** within the joint limits
(default: 200 000 samples across 4 096 parallel environments).  For each
sample the Isaac Sim physics engine computes the forward kinematics and
the geometric Jacobian at the end-effector body.  A gripper-tip offset
of (0, 0, −0.225) m along the EE body's local Z axis accounts for the
Robotiq 2F-140 fingertip position.

### 1.2  Quality Measures

Three complementary quality measures are computed per sample and
aggregated into voxels (default voxel size: 20 mm):

**Yoshikawa Manipulability Index** (Yoshikawa, 1985):

$$w(\mathbf{q}) = \sqrt{\det\!\bigl(J(\mathbf{q})\, J(\mathbf{q})^T\bigr)}$$

Higher values indicate the end effector can move equally well in all
Cartesian directions (far from singularities).  Zero at singular
configurations.

**Inverse Condition Number** (isotropy index; Salisbury & Craig, 1982):

$$\kappa^{-1} = \frac{\sigma_{\min}}{\sigma_{\max}} \quad \in [0, 1]$$

where $\sigma_{\min}$ and $\sigma_{\max}$ are the smallest and largest
singular values of $J$.  A value of 1 represents perfectly isotropic
motion capability.

**Reachability Density**: the number of FK samples landing in each
voxel, proportional to the joint-space volume mapping to that Cartesian
region.  Higher density means more configurations can reach that area.

### 1.3  Desired Workspace

The desired workspace is derived from the Klein (2023) task geometry:

| Axis | Min (m) | Max (m) |
|------|---------|---------|
| X | −0.05 | +0.35 |
| Y | −0.40 | +0.95 |
| Z | +0.80 | +1.30 |

A translucent box visualises this volume during sampling.

### 1.4  Supported Robots

| Key | Robot | DOF | End-Effector Body | Mount Height |
|-----|-------|-----|-------------------|-------------|
| `tensegrity` | 5-DOF Tensegrity Robot (elbow_approx) | 5 (2 base + 3 arm) | `tool_link_0` | 2.30 m |
| `tensegrity_physical` | 5-DOF Tensegrity Robot (physical 4-bar) | 6 (2 base + 2 rod + 2 wrist)¹ | `tool_link_0` | 2.30 m |
| `ur10e` | UR10e + Robotiq 2F-140 | 6 | `robotiq_base_link` | 1.40 m |
| `kinova` | Kinova Gen3 + Robotiq 2F-140 | 7 | `end_effector_link` | 1.40 m |

¹ The four-bar linkage has 1 kinematic DOF.  `rod_right_joint` and
`coupler_left_joint` are computed from `rod_left_joint` via the
McCarthy & Soh (2010) antiparallelogram closure equation during sampling.

### 1.5  Visualisation

`workspace_visualize.py` generates publication-quality figures for each
quality measure.  Each figure contains:

- **Left**: 3-D voxelised scatter plot (colour-mapped by metric value)
- **Right**: three 2-D cross-section heatmaps — thin slices through the
  midpoint of the desired workspace along the X, Y, and Z axes

An interactive Jupyter notebook (`workspace_analysis.ipynb`) is also
available for exploratory analysis.

---

## 2  Running the Analysis

All commands are run from the `src/tensegrity_pick` directory.

### Step 1 — Sample workspace

```bash
conda run --no-capture-output -n env_isaaclab python3 -u \
    scripts/workspace_analysis/workspace_sample.py --headless
```

Optional flags:

| Flag | Default | Description |
|------|---------|-------------|
| `--robot` | `tensegrity` | Robot to analyse (`tensegrity`, `tensegrity_physical`, `ur10e`, `kinova`) |
| `--num_samples` | 200 000 | Total FK samples |
| `--num_envs` | 4 096 | Parallel environments |
| `--mount_height` | per-robot | Override mount height (m) |

Data is written to `outputs/workspace_analysis_<robot>/` as NumPy files.

### Step 2 — Visualise

```bash
python3 scripts/workspace_analysis/workspace_visualize.py
python3 scripts/workspace_analysis/workspace_visualize.py --voxel_size 0.03
```

Plots are saved to the same output directory.

---

## 3  Results

### 3.1  Tensegrity Robot (elbow_approx)

**Configuration:** 2,000,000 Monte Carlo FK samples, ceiling-mounted at 2.30 m, 4 096 parallel environments.

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Total samples | 2,000,000 |
| Samples inside desired workspace | 702,277 (35.1 %) |
| Desired workspace coverage | 99.68 % (33,890 / 34,000 voxels) |
| Yoshikawa manipulability (inside WS, mean) | 5.2 × 10⁻⁵ |
| Yoshikawa manipulability (inside WS, median) | 1 × 10⁻⁶ |
| Yoshikawa manipulability (inside WS, p95) | 1.91 × 10⁻⁴ |

The elbow_approx model achieves near-complete coverage of the desired workspace (99.7 %). Manipulability is highest near the centre of the workspace and drops toward the boundaries, as expected for the 5-DOF kinematic chain.

![Tensegrity workspace density](../Figures/Workspace/tensegrity_workspace_density.png)
![Tensegrity workspace manipulability](../Figures/Workspace/tensegrity_workspace_manipulability.png)

### 3.2  Tensegrity Robot (physical four-bar linkage)

**Configuration:** 2,000,000 samples requested; 1,957,773 survived after
the antiparallelogram closure condition, body-chain integrity validation,
and physics-bounds filtering.
Ceiling-mounted at 2.30 m, 256 parallel environments.

The physical model enforces the antiparallelogram closure equation:
`rod_right_joint` and `coupler_left_joint` are computed analytically from
`rod_left_joint` using the McCarthy & Soh (2010) four-bar closure condition
(see `antiparallelogram_kinematics.ipynb`).  The rod joint limits are
derived from the ±70° elbow range: `rod_left ∈ [−0.6886, +0.5332]` rad,
`rod_right ∈ [−0.5332, +0.6886]` rad.

To ensure the PhysX loop-closure constraint (`coupler_right_joint`,
`excludeFromArticulation`) remains intact, the robot is reset to its
initial joint configuration before each sampling batch.  Post-FK body-chain
validation rejects samples where adjacent body origins are more than 0.5 m
apart (indicating a broken constraint).

| Metric | Value |
|--------|-------|
| Total samples | 1,957,773 |
| Samples inside desired workspace | 633,755 (32.4 %) |
| Desired workspace coverage | 99.01 % (33,664 / 34,000 voxels) |
| Yoshikawa manipulability (inside WS, mean) | 6.2 × 10⁻⁵ |
| Yoshikawa manipulability (inside WS, median) | 1 × 10⁻⁶ |
| Yoshikawa manipulability (inside WS, p95) | 2.31 × 10⁻⁴ |

The physical model achieves comparable workspace coverage (99.7 %) to the
elbow_approx model.  The workspace is now symmetric about the Y axis, as
expected from the kinematic structure, confirming that the closure condition
correctly constrains the four-bar linkage.

![Physical workspace density](../Figures/Workspace/physical_workspace_density.png)
![Physical workspace manipulability](../Figures/Workspace/physical_workspace_manipulability.png)

### 3.3  Comparison

| Metric | elbow_approx | physical |
|--------|-------------|----------|
| Desired WS coverage | 99.68 % | 99.01 % |
| Samples in desired WS | 35.1 % | 32.4 % |
| Manipulability mean (in WS) | 5.2 × 10⁻⁵ | 6.2 × 10⁻⁵ |
| Manipulability p95 (in WS) | 1.91 × 10⁻⁴ | 2.31 × 10⁻⁴ |

Both models cover >99 % of the desired workspace with near-identical
manipulability statistics.  The physical model produces a symmetric
workspace, confirming the correctness of the antiparallelogram closure
equation.  The slightly lower coverage (99.0 % vs 99.7 %) is due to the
body-chain integrity filter rejecting ~2 % of samples where the PhysX
loop-closure constraint was marginally degraded.

